Chaos Master wrote:

> Just started a translation of NetHack 3.4.3 to Portuguese.
> 
> The bitch part will be changing the code that generates potions' and
> objects' "name" as in:
> 
> x - a +2 <adjective> <item> , in portuguese this would be 'a +2 <item>
> <adjective>' (e.g. "white potion" = "pocao branca", "small shield" =
> "escudo pequeno").

I can offer you no help in translation, as I can barely put subject and verb 
together in Portuguese; but if you're using the Spanish code, I can answer 
any questions you may have about how it works.

One problem you're going to face that doesn't exist in Spanish is forming 
the plural of a noun in -ão.  As many times as the code calls 
body_part(HAND) and passes the result to makeplural(), I'm sure you can 
understand that you want to get mãos and not mões or mães.  You may need to 
treat the most common case as a rule and the rest as irregular forms.

The character-set mods ought to work unchanged.  Spanish Nethack uses 
ISO-8859-1 for its internal character set and provides functions to do 
correct capitalization, matches without accents (so e.g. you can wish for 
that "pocao branca" even though it's "poção branca" inside the code) and to 
match the character set to the output device.  The various grammar-related 
routines will be useful, though they may need some tweaks in the details of 
their working.  I used the al() and del() functions to create those 
contractions in the right places; your versions will be rather more 
complex, as you have ao, numa, pelo, duns, etc. where Spanish has only al 
and del.

Finally, don't take my Spanish translations as gospel.  I'm a gringo, and 
although several native speakers have reviewed much of the code, I'm sure 
there's still a mistranslation or fifty in there.

